<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">British Seychelles</h1><p class="st-meta">1810 CE – 1976 CE · capital Victoria</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Indian Ocean archipelago held by Britain from the Napoleonic Wars until 1976, a plantation colony and a place of exile.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The French-settled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seychelles">Seychelles</a> surrendered to British warships several times during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>, while the French commandant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queau-de-quinssy">Quéau de Quinssy</a> stayed on to run the islands. After the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a> in 1810 they passed under British control, confirmed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1814">Treaty of Paris</a> in 1814, and were governed as a dependency of Mauritius.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>French-speaking planters grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and then coconuts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cinnamon">cinnamon</a> and vanilla with enslaved labour until abolition in 1835; Africans freed from slave ships by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> were later landed there. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> society formed, mostly Catholic. The islands became a separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crown-colony">Crown colony</a> in 1903 with its capital at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-victoria">Victoria</a>, and served as a place of exile for figures such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prempeh-i">Prempeh I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makarios-iii">Archbishop Makarios</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After internal self-government in 1970, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seychelles">Seychelles</a> became independent on 29 June 1976 as a republic within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commonwealth-of-nations">Commonwealth</a> under President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-mancham">James Mancham</a>, whose government was overthrown in a coup the next year by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france-albert-rene">France-Albert René</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/british-seychelles.jpg" alt="British Seychelles" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Gerard Larose, Seychelles Tourism Board · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seychelles_History_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1810 CE – 1976 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Victoria</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Quéau de Quinssy, James Mancham</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Seychellois Creole, English, French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Seychelles</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Colony of Seychelles, Seychelles</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1893&amp;focus=british-seychelles" aria-label="British Seychelles on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6218 2351 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where British Seychelles lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6538 2607L4997 651M6538 2607L6550 3180M6538 2607L6550 3180"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0M6550 3180h0M6550 3180h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6538 2607h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6538 2607h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Victoria. <a href="/atlas?year=1893&amp;focus=british-seychelles">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
